WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.929
Film Courage: How are you softening some of
the screenwriters you’re working with who

00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:09.540
are either too defensive or too much of a
namby-pamby “yes” person?

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:13.600
Lee Jessup: It is a balancing act.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:19.730
I think once writers start getting notes and
a lot of notes, they learn…they learn.

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:24.880
Because you’re going to get notes that are
outright offensive to you.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.400
But you learn to take the good with the bad.

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.050
I always tell writers as they prepare for
the industry, go find people who will hate

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:36.350
your work, those are the people whose notes
you want.

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.300
You don't want the people who will tell you
how brilliant you are, how perfect it is.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:41.150
You’re not going to learn anything from
that.

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:45.200
You’re going to learn how to take notes
from getting notes that are really offensive

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.960
and finding something in there that you can
use.

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.340
What is the core of that?

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:55.100
What is the little kernel inside that note
that you can take and do something with and

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:57.340
really move the material forward with?

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.560
And listen, I remember this from my days from
getting notes as a writer and getting infuriated

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.040
and coming back and going “Okay, there was
something there.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.640
What was that thing that was there?

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.760
What is the note giver really saying?”

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:14.369
So you want really to teach writers to take
notes, to be receptive to finding that kernel,

00:01:14.369 --> 00:01:18.200
that truth, that is in every note or in most
notes.

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:22.619
You also want to work with writers the way
that I work with writers and that is in not

00:01:22.619 --> 00:01:25.619
giving them the answers when they are like
“Okay, here is this note so I am going to

00:01:25.619 --> 00:01:27.810
execute it that way…Is that good?”

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:28.810
No!

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:32.340
Figure out your way to execute that note and
I get writers coming back and going “There

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:36.700
was one note that said this, how do I implement
it?”

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:37.859
You figure it out.

00:01:37.859 --> 00:01:39.340
You’re on your own.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:44.240
I’ll sit with you and I’ll brainstorm
with you once you have the direction but it

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.530
has to be your direction.

00:01:45.530 --> 00:01:48.899
And that is where you have to listen to your
inner writer.

00:01:48.899 --> 00:01:52.579
You have to really foster your talent, your
sensibilities.

00:01:52.579 --> 00:01:54.819
Of course it has to be malleable.

00:01:54.819 --> 00:01:58.970
You have to be able to work with other people’s
sensibilities, but the key to being a great

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:02.469
writer is taking other people’s ideas and
making them your own.

00:02:02.469 --> 00:02:06.780
And falling just as in love with them and
being just as invested in them as you would

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:13.019
be had your idea or concept originated with
you because the reality today is that 2014,

00:02:13.019 --> 00:02:19.120
1,800 WGA members made income in the film
industry.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:21.510
Roughly 132 scripts sold.

00:02:21.510 --> 00:02:22.510
What does that mean?

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:28.500
That means that over 1,600 people made their
money writing pages, doing writing assignments.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.060
So how do you become that writer who does
writing assignments and does it successfully?

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.980
You’re able to take a kernel of an idea
and make it your own and fall in love with

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:37.980
it.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:41.349
And really take it and run with it in a way
that makes other people want to work with

00:02:41.349 --> 00:02:42.349
you.

00:02:42.349 --> 00:02:42.365
Question for the Viewers: Do you give your
script to people who will tear it apart?

